<p><a href="/api/media/file/BOE_Agenda_2026_07_22_11d79f5f21.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda<span class="sr-only"> about July 22, 2026 Board of Equalization of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-594" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Community Development Block Grant Public Notice</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">July 20, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="/api/media/file/Draft_update_AAP_PY_26_Public_Notice_f5578dde07.docx" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Notice<span class="sr-only"> about Community Development Block Grant Public Notice (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-597" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Youth Soccer National Championship becomes economic driver for Utah County</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">July 20, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>PROVO, Utah — The US Youth Soccer National Championships have descended on Utah Valley, turning Epic Sports Park into the largest event the venue has ever hosted. Organizers say more than 4,000 players are competing across 287 teams, with the games played at Epic’s fields in Utah County over the next ten days. </p>
<p>We spoke with Abby Owens of Explore Utah Valley, who said the event has filled hotels across the region and brought a flood of visitors to local restaurants, shops and the Provo Airport, where welcome signage greets teams arriving for their stay. </p>
<p>“It’s the biggest thing we’ve ever hosted,” Owens said. “It’s the largest event yet for Epic Sports Park.” </p>
<p>The championship’s growth is partly the product of pursuing bigger events and aiming high. It doesn’t get any bigger than going up against Disney.  Owens was part of a team that brought the championships from Orlando to Provo. </p>
<p>“Just to see what we could do,” Owens recalled of the visit to Orlando, where she and others met with CEOs and event managers. The goal, she said, was to elevate Epic Sports Park to a level that could compete with Disney World, “if not better,” she noted with a wink about the lofty aim.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-588" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Notice of Proposed Tax Increases-Utah County Combined Notice</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">July 20, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>This is a notice of a tax increase being proposed by several taxing entities to increase property tax revenue within Utah County. All concerned citizens are invited to the public hearings for the purpose of hearing comments regarding the proposed tax increases and to explain the reasons for the proposed tax increase. Use the link below to see the full list of taxing entities considering increases, the amount of additional revenue the increase is intended to generate, and what the estimated tax increase will be on the average home and business.</p>
<h4><a href="/api/media/file/2026_T_in_T_Ad_Utah_Co_Combined_Proof_e023967a5f.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>List of Taxing Entity Public Hearings</strong><span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></h4></div></div></article><article id="notice-515" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Community Development Block Grant Public Notice</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">July 13, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="/api/media/file/Spring_Lake_FONSI_and_NOI_RROF_002_6be1fd2583.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Notice<span class="sr-only"> about Community Development Block Grant Public Notice (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-593" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Employee of The Month July 2026</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">July 9, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Deputy Carson, the School Resource Officer at Sage Canyon Middle School in Eagle Mountain, is a model of dedication and professionalism. She prioritizes student safety and well-being in every aspect of her daily duties, maintaining a visible presence and building strong relationships with students and staff. Her goal is to provide a safe learning environment where students feel supported.  She strives to be a positive role model and trusted resource beyond enforcement. </p>
<p>Deputy Carson also shines in investigations, taking the time to ensure each case is thoroughly and fairly resolved. Her attention to detail, persistence, and commitment reflects the highest standards of law enforcement. She regularly assists fellow deputies, lending support across school incidents and patrol duties, underscoring exceptional teamwork and dependability. </p>
<p>For these reasons, Deputy Carson embodies outstanding professional conduct and is deserving of July’s Employee of the Month.   </p>
<p>Congratulations Deputy Carson!</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-502" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">2026 Freedom Festival Highlights!</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">July 8, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>The 2026 Freedom Festival and all the surrounding events have come to an end!  Below is a video highlighting all the awesome events we were a part of and a message from the Commissioners on what Independence Day means to Utah County, especially during this 250 year celebration.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-592" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">The 2026 America’s Freedom Festival Parade Draws More Than 350,000 Spectators</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">July 7, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>PROVO, Utah — The 2026 America’s Freedom Festival Parade drew more than 350,000 spectators on July 4, cementing its status as one of the largest Independence Day celebrations in the United States and in the Western United States, organizers said.</p>
<p>The highly anticipated parade featured more than 100 entries, historic floats and military displays, bringing massive crowds to University Avenue in downtown Provo. Die-hard attendees regularly camp out along the parade route the day before to secure premium viewing spots.</p>
<p>The festival generates more than $25.7 million in annual economic impact for the local economy, officials said, underscoring its role as an economic engine for the region. As the busiest week of the year for Provo and surrounding cities, the multi-day festival drives activity across tourism, hospitality and downtown commerce.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-416" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">July 8, 2026 Board of Equalization of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">July 7, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="https://agenda.utahcounty.gov/agenda?id=1992" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda with Documents<span class="sr-only"> about July 8, 2026 Board of Equalization of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/BOE_Agenda_2026_07_08_bbfda0d04c.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda<span class="sr-only"> about July 8, 2026 Board of Equalization of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-355" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">July 8, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">July 6, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="https://agenda.utahcounty.gov/agenda?id=1894" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda with Documents<span class="sr-only"> about July 8, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/Commission_Agenda_2026_07_08_4651504b40.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda<span class="sr-only"> about July 8, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-591" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Freedom Balloon Festival lights up Provo skies</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">July 6, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>PROVO, Utah — The Freedom Balloon Festival, an annual Fourth of July tradition in Provo, drew more than 25 hot air balloons to Fox Field for three morning launches, a Friday night Balloon Glow and pilot competitions.</p>
<p>Residents and visitors awoke before dawn to watch the balloons inflate and take off, with many saying the experience is a staple of local summer traditions. “I grew up here in Provo and this is a staple for a lot of families. You get up early before the sun comes up. Get down here, get a good spot and watch the balloons inflate and take off,” County Commissioner Amelia Powers Gardner said.</p>
<p>The festival featured three morning launches, a Friday night Balloon Glow and pilot competitions.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-21" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Community Development Block Grant Public Notice</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">July 1, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="/api/media/file/FONSI_RROF_for_The_Refuge_db8da71e0f.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Notice<span class="sr-only"> about Community Development Block Grant Public Notice (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-585" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">July 7, 2026 Board of County Canvassers of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 30, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>PUBLIC NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN THAT</p>
<p><strong>BOARD OF COUNTY CANVASSERS OF UTAH COUNTY, UTAH</strong></p>
<p>WILL HOLD A PUBLIC MEETING IN THE</p>
<p>UTAH COUNTY COMMISSION CHAMBERS – ROOM 1400</p>
<p>OF THE UTAH COUNTY ADMINISTRATION BUILDING</p>
<p>100 EAST CENTER STREET, PROVO, UTAH 84606</p>
<p><strong>July 7, 2026– 2:00 pm</strong></p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/07_07_2026_Board_of_Canvassers_3dce518a20.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Agenda<span class="sr-only"> about July 7, 2026 Board of County Canvassers of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-550" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">July 1, 2026 - Post-Election Audit</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 29, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Notice of Post-Election Audit<br/>
<!-- -->In accordance with Utah Code §§ 20A-4-104(1) and 20A-1-108(1), the Utah County Elections Division will conduct a post-election audit of the 2026 Primary Election on Wednesday July 1, 2026, at 10:00 am. Anyone desiring to witness or participate in the audit is invited to attend. The audit will be conducted at the following location:</p>
<p>Utah County Administration Building<br/>
<!-- -->Ballot Processing Center<br/>
<!-- -->100 E Center Street, Room LL700<br/>
<!-- -->Provo, UT 84606</p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/Notice_of_Post_Election_Audit_207b46b4a6.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Notice<span class="sr-only"> about July 1, 2026 - Post-Election Audit (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-590" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Employee of the Month June 2026</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 25, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Congratulations Diana Holley for being named June 2026 Employee of the Month!</p>
<p>Diana has worked for the Recorder’s Office for 20+ years, starting in customer service and then moving into mapping, where she became the mapping supervisor about three years ago. She is friendly and helpful with a smile, yet can be no-nonsense when needed and will stand up for the people and ideals she believes in, demanding respect when the occasion warrants. </p>
<p>Service-oriented and committed to helping everyone be their best, she is quick to praise what’s done well and handles corrections with kindness, all while keeping the atmosphere light as a morale booster and the type of person you’d like to work for. </p>
<p>She also takes the time to listen to questions, guides colleagues to clear answers, goes above and beyond to explain procedures, ensures everyone understand when new procedures are released, and works hard to keep trainings current so everyone is on the same page to help our customers the most.</p>
<p>Congratulations Diana!  Your commitment to the Recorders&#x27;s Office and Utah County is evident in your unwavering public service, meticulous attention to accuracy, and steadfast support for residents and colleagues alike.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-589" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">June 26, 2026 Utah County Criminal Justice Coordinating Council Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 24, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>PUBLIC NOTICE IS HEARBY GIVEN THAT THE UTAH COUNTY CRIMINAL JUSTICE COORDINATING COUNCIL WILL HOLD A PUBLIC MEETING AT 51 SOUTH UNIVERSITY AVE., PROVO, UTAH 84601 IN THE HISTORIC COURTHOUSE, 3rd FLOOR COMMISSION CONFERENCE ROOM </p>
<p>Friday, June 26, 2026 – 12:00 p.m. </p>
<p>Meeting Link </p>
<p>When necessary, board members may participate electronically, with the anchor location as stated above. </p>
<p>The public may participate at the anchor location stated above. Public comments will be limited to two (2) minutes per individual unless otherwise approved by the Board. </p>
<p>AGENDA<br/>
<!-- -->1. Welcome<br/>
<!-- -->-Jeff Gray<br/>
<!-- -->2. Approve previous meeting minutes<br/>
<!-- -->3. Open and Public Meetings Act Training<br/>
<!-- -->-Ben Van Noy<br/>
<!-- -->4. Discuss status of pretrial services<br/>
<!-- -->5. Discuss Council goals and next projects<br/>
<!-- -->6. Next Meeting Date &amp; Adjourn<br/>
<!-- -->PUBLIC COMMENTS</p>
<p>In compliance with the American with Disabilities Act, individuals needing special accommodations (including communicative aids and services) during this meeting should notify Jolynn Clegg at (801) 851-8113 at least three days prior to the meeting. Board members may participate electronically as needed, with the anchor location for the public meeting to be the same as listed above.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-538" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Utah County wins Best Innovative Use of Technology Award</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 23, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>The state of Utah recognized Utah County as the winner of the Best Innovative Use of Technology Award for a project called AI-Driven Storm Drain Detection for Mosquito Abatement. It’s a very cool project that leveraged AI to identify storm drains. By using AI, Utah County nearly doubled the number of drains they previously knew about. </p>
<p>What makes this win so exciting? It’s a bold leap from manual labor to smart technology in the fight against West Nile virus. West Nile prevention in urban Utah counties hinges on eliminating mosquito breeding sites from storm drains. But cataloging tens of thousands of drainage structures across sprawling municipalities has long been an overwhelming logistical challenge. This project flips the script by using artificial intelligence to automatically identify storm drains from high-resolution aerial imagery.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-521" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">June 24, 2026 Board of Equalization of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 23, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="https://agenda.utahcounty.gov/agenda?id=1991" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda with Documents<span class="sr-only"> about June 24, 2026 Board of Equalization of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/BOE_Agenda_2026_06_24_c3608d2f7f.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda<span class="sr-only"> about June 24, 2026 Board of Equalization of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-513" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">June 24, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 22, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="https://agenda.utahcounty.gov/agenda?id=1893" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda with Documents<span class="sr-only"> about June 24, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/Commission_Agenda_2026_06_24_beb56b053c.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda<span class="sr-only"> about June 24, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-499" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Young Washington Advanced Screening for Utah County</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 17, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Young Washington is coming to a theater near you on 4th of July weekend. Angel Studios provided Utah County with an advanced screening in celebration of the 250th anniversary of our nation. In the film, young George Washington faces war, betrayal, and impossible choices that will forge a leader.</p>
<p>This film explores the courage, sacrifice, and destiny that forged a legendary leader. With breathtaking cinematography, gripping drama, and historical intensity, this new film reveals the untold struggle that would change the fate of the world forever.  It was a great watch!</p>
<p>Special thanks to the Orem Public Library for screening the film.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-559" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Sample Ballot for the June 23, 2026 Primary Election</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 17, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="/api/media/file/2026_Primary_NEW_Sample_Ballot_4b4ee98334.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Ballot<span class="sr-only"> about Sample Ballot for the June 23, 2026 Primary Election (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-549" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">America 250 Freedom Festival Utah&#x27; Prayer Breakfast</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 16, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>The Freedom Festival’s Prayer Breakfast, held in Provo, Utah is a nondenominational gathering that unites community members, faith groups, and civic leaders in prayer for America and her leaders.  </p>
<p>The audience enjoyed inspiring remarks from keynote speaker, Utah Attorney General Derek Brown, uplifting music, and a chance to reflect on faith, unity, and the values that strengthen the United States of America.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-537" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">June 18, 2026 Utah County Capital Improvement Plan Committee Public Work Session</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 15, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>PUBLIC NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN THAT THE </p>
<p><strong>UTAH COUNTY CAPITAL IMPROVEMENT PLAN COMMITTEE</strong></p>
<p>WILL HOLD A PUBLIC ONLINE ONLY WORK SESSION</p>
<p>LINK: </p>
<p><a href="https://teams.microsoft.com/meet/294388031901732?p=rdMmfBs9fygd1IW0M7" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span class="sr-only"> about June 18, 2026 Utah County Capital Improvement Plan Committee Public Work Session (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p>
<p>ON WEDNESDAY, JUNE 18, 2026 AT 8:30AM.</p>
<p><strong>WORK SESSION AGENDA</strong></p>
<p><strong>1.</strong>         <strong>REVIEW PROPOSED AMENDMENTS TO THE 2026-2028 CIP PLAN</strong></p>
<p>In compliance with the Americans with Disabilities Act, individuals needing special accommodations (including auxiliary communicative aids and services) during this meeting should notify the Utah County Clerk’s Office at (801) 851-8113. </p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/CIP_Public_Meeting_06_18_2026_77a19b9351.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Agenda<span class="sr-only"> about June 18, 2026 Utah County Capital Improvement Plan Committee Public Work Session (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-558" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Community Development Block Grant Public Notice</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 15, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="/api/media/file/UTCO_CP_Public_Notice_for_July_2026_07a3d52638.docx" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Notice<span class="sr-only"> about Community Development Block Grant Public Notice (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-548" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Utah County State Stage 1 Restrictions</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 12, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Due to current and forecasted weather conditions coupled with the extremely dry vegetation conditions in Utah County, the State Forester, in consultation with the County Commission of the affected county, has determined that measures must be taken to prevent the ignition of forest and rangeland fires.  </p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/2026_Utah_County_State_Stage_1_Restrictions_signed_202606101213326759_ec448ce40f.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Notice<span class="sr-only"> about Utah County State Stage 1 Restrictions (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-583" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">June 11, 2026  Free Public Town Hall Elections Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 9, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>ENSURE YOUR VOTE IS COUNTED: TECHNICAL ERRORS THAT THREATEN YOUNG VOTERS</p>
<p>Provo, UT (June 8, 2026) – Utah County Clerk Aaron Davidson will host a free public Town Hall on Thursday, June 11 at 7:00 PM in the Utah County Commissioners Chambers to address a growing concern: a significant number of first‑time voter ballots are not being counted. With the June 23 primary election approaching and the voter registration deadline this Friday, Clerk Davidson is urging voters—especially young voters—to take simple steps now to ensure their ballots are counted.</p>
<p>When a mail‑in ballot envelope is missing a signature or the signature does not match what is on file, the ballot is flagged for the signature‑cure process. Election officials then contact the voter to verify the signature. The system protects voters, but only works when voters respond quickly. Many do not see the notice or miss the deadline, resulting in valid ballots going uncounted.</p>
<p>Young voters are disproportionately affected. In the 2024 General Election, voters ages 18–25 accounted for 55.46% of all ballots that were challenged and not cured. Among voters ages 18–21, 13.46% of all ballots cast were challenged—roughly one in seven. These ballots are legitimate votes from eligible Utahns who participated but were blocked by a preventable issue.</p>
<p>“Every eligible vote deserves to be counted,” said Clerk Davidson. “Young voters are showing up, but small technical issues are standing between them and having their voice heard. With the primary approaching, now is the time to act.”</p>
<p>The June 11 Town Hall will explain how to check ballot status, how the curing process works, and what voters can do immediately to ensure their ballot is counted. The event is free, open to the public, and requires no registration. Voters ages 18–25 and their parents are especially encouraged to attend.</p>
<p>Location: Utah County Commissioners Chambers, 100 East Center Street, Provo, Utah 84606. For those unable to attend in person, the Town Hall will also be livestreamed: Utah County Elections - YouTube.</p>
<p>The Utah County Clerk’s Office administers elections for more than 371,000 registered voters and is committed to transparent, accessible, and accurate elections. For information on ballot status or curing, contact the Utah County Elections Division.</p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/Press_release_June_11th_Town_Hall_Utah_County_Clerk_1_48c45ad86f.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Notice<span class="sr-only"> about June 11, 2026  Free Public Town Hall Elections Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-536" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">The Chosen Boosts Local Economy </h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 9, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>GOSHEN, Utah — The set of the television series The Chosen has become a focal point in Utah County, with a biblical village in Goshen built by the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints and production crews turning the landscape into a stand-in for biblical-era scenes. </p>
<p>On the ground, the set is both impressive and welcoming due in part to an incredible production designer and art team.  Utah became central to the project when President and Executive Producer, Brad Pelo called Dallas Jenkins, the series creator. “I reached out and invited him to meet me in Utah and showed him not only the landscape, but we came here to Goshen. He said, ‘This is the place. This is where I would love to tell my stories.’ ” </p>
<p>Utah emerged as the series’ second home, with production starting on season two, underscoring the state’s growing role in the show’s development and production.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-379" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">June 10, 2026 Board of Equalization of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 9, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="https://agenda.utahcounty.gov/agenda?id=1990" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda with Documents<span class="sr-only"> about June 10, 2026 Board of Equalization of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/BOE_Agenda_2026_06_10_9ea40c80f2.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda<span class="sr-only"> about June 10, 2026 Board of Equalization of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-353" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">June 10, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 8, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="https://agenda.utahcounty.gov/agenda?id=1892" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda with Documents<span class="sr-only"> about June 10, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/Commission_Agenda_2026_06_10_efbb43b6b1.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda<span class="sr-only"> about June 10, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-584" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Commissioner Beltran Participates in “Come to the Table Homeless Experience”</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 8, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>UTAH COUNTY, Utah — Utah County Commissioner Skyler Beltran helped kick off Community Action Services and Food Bank’s annual “Come to the Table Homeless Experience” this evening, an immersive program designed to help community members better understand the challenges faced by individuals experiencing homelessness.</p>
<p>The event brings together service providers, service missionaries, community leaders, and members of the public to participate in a realistic simulation of navigating homelessness. Each participant is assigned a profile reflecting circumstances commonly experienced by individuals in the community and then spends the evening accessing services much like someone facing homelessness would.</p>
<p>Participants visit local service providers, receive a sack dinner from the Food and Care Coalition, utilize public transportation, and navigate various support systems available throughout the community.</p>
<p>During his remarks, Commissioner Beltran highlighted the collaborative efforts between Utah County, the State of Utah, nonprofit organizations, faith-based groups, and community partners to connect individuals with the resources they need to improve their circumstances.</p>
<p>“Utah County is a compassionate community. Utah County is a service-oriented community, and Utah County is an effective community,” said Commissioner Beltran. “If you want to improve your situation and get better, we have the resources and people to help you get there.”</p>
<p>The “Come to the Table Homeless Experience” aims to increase awareness and strengthen partnerships among those working to address homelessness while highlighting the extensive network of services available to individuals and families in need.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-347" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Planning Commission Meeting and Notice of Public Hearing June 16, 2026</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 5, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>The Utah County Planning Commission will hold a public hearing at its regular meeting on <strong>June 16th, 2026, at 5:30 p.m. in Room 1400 of the Utah County Administration Building, 100 East Center Street, Provo, Utah</strong></p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/Agenda_2026_June_16_e0fad84ecf.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Agenda<span class="sr-only"> about Planning Commission Meeting and Notice of Public Hearing June 16, 2026 (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/Public_Hearing_6_16_26_7c15729d0e.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Public Notice<span class="sr-only"> about Planning Commission Meeting and Notice of Public Hearing June 16, 2026 (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-582" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Utah County Jail&#x27;s Food Handler Permit Program Gives Inmates a Real Shot at Success </h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 4, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><strong>A kitchen, a skill, a second chance</strong> </p>
<p>Walk into the Utah County Jail kitchen and you&#x27;ll find something most people don&#x27;t expect: hope. The jail feeds its inmates, its staff and its work teams, but it&#x27;s more than just a place to cook meals. It&#x27;s become a launchpad for inmates looking to turn their lives around. </p>
<p>Last year, jail officials launched a food handler&#x27;s permit program. The idea was simple but powerful: teach inmates real skills they can use when they get out. In the past two months alone, eight inmates have earned their food handler&#x27;s permit...four last month, four the month before. </p>
<p>&quot;If we can give them a leg up and help them go and apply for jobs, that&#x27;s been helpful for some guys,&quot; said Deputy Sheriff Chris Nielson. </p>
<p><strong>More Than Just Cooking</strong> </p>
<p>The training goes deep. Inmates learn how to work in a commercial-grade food kitchen, master food safety protocols and understand equipment safety. But the real curriculum is about character. </p>
<p>&quot;We work on teaching them work ethic, accountability, responsibility,&quot; Nielson explained. The goal isn&#x27;t just to teach someone how to cook. It&#x27;s to build confidence. It&#x27;s to give them a taste of success.” </p>
<p><strong>Dignity Changes Everything</strong> </p>
<p>The philosophy here is straightforward: well-fed inmates and well-respected inmates are well-behaved inmates.  &quot;They&#x27;re people, right? And they deserve to be treated with dignity and respect,&quot; Nielson emphasized.  The kitchen&#x27;s menu proves it. The meals served in the café range from basic to surprisingly sophisticated. It&#x27;s a far cry from the stereotype of prison food.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-462" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Notice of Vacancy - North Pointe Solid Waste Special Service District Control Board</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 1, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>NOTICE OF VACANCY ON THE NORTH POINTE SOLID WASTE SPECIAL SERVICE DISTRICT CONTROL BOARD</p>
<p>Notice is hereby provided this 27th day of May 2026, pursuant to Utah Code Section 17B-1-304 that one (1) vacancy currently exist on the North Pointe Solid Waste Special Service District Control Board (the “Board”). To be qualified for consideration for the abovementioned Board vacancy, individuals must be a registered voter within the boundaries of the North Pointe Solid Waste Special Service District (the “District”).</p>
<p>Those individuals interested in being considered for the vacant Board position outlined above should send a letter expressing their interest, together with a brief description of their qualifications and eligibility for the Board position to: <a href="mailto:boards@utahcounty.gov">boards@utahcounty.gov</a>. All letters of interest in the vacant board positions must be received by the Commission, on or before 5:00 p.m. on Friday, July 10th, 2026. The Board of County Commissioners, Utah County, Utah, will review and evaluate all individuals that qualify for consideration pursuant to the guidelines listed above and will appoint an individual to fill the existing vacancy by resolution.</p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/Notice_2026_406_ca46b26d80.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Notice<span class="sr-only"> about Notice of Vacancy - North Pointe Solid Waste Special Service District Control Board (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-567" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Notice of Vacancy - South Utah Valley Animal Services Special Service District Administrative Control Board</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">June 1, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>NOTICE OF VACANCY ON THE SOUTH UTAH VALLEY ANIMAL SERVICES SPECIAL SERVICE DISTRICT ADMINISTRATIVE CONTROL BOARD</p>
<p>Notice is hereby provided this 27th day of May 2026, pursuant to Utah Code Section 17B-1-304 that one (1) vacancy currently exist on the South Utah Valley Animal Services Special Service District Administrative Control Board (the “Board”). To be qualified for<br/>
<!-- -->consideration for the above-mentioned Board vacancy, individuals must be a registered voter within the boundaries of the South Utah Valley Animal Services Special Service District (the “District”) or an officer or employee of Utah County.</p>
<p>Those individuals interested in being considered for the vacant Board position outlined above should send a letter expressing their interest, together with a brief description of their qualifications and eligibility for the Board position to: <a href="mailto:boards@utahcounty.gov">boards@utahcounty.gov</a>. All letters of interest in the vacant board positions must be received by the Commission, on or before 5:00 p.m. on Friday, July 10th, 2026. The Board of County Commissioners, Utah County, Utah, will review and evaluate all individuals that qualify for consideration pursuant to the guidelines listed above and will appoint an individual to fill the existing Board vacancy by resolution.</p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/Notice_2026_407_0b0bc6b1ce.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Notice<span class="sr-only"> about Notice of Vacancy - South Utah Valley Animal Services Special Service District Administrative Control Board (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-361" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">&quot;I Voted&quot; 250 Sticker Contest Winners</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">May 27, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Big congratulations to the three winners of our &quot;I Voted&quot; 250 Sticker Contest.  The winning entry is the official “I Voted” sticker for the November 2026 General Election! The contest was open to all Utah County students in grades 5–9.</p>
<p>1st Place: Ezra Nguyen - $150 + design featured countywide </p>
<p>2nd Place: McKenzie Stevens - $100 </p>
<p>3rd Place: Eden Lynn Frisby - $50 </p>
<p>Their creativity stood out from the crowd, and they were recognized and rewarded at our recent commission meeting. </p>
<p>The winners were chosen by elected officials in Utah County and by city recorders. Thanks to everyone who participated and celebrated the power of voting!</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-263" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">May 27, 2026 Utah County Community Reinvestment Agency Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">May 26, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>PUBLIC NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN THAT THE <strong>UTAH COUNTY COMMUNITY REINVESTMENT AGENCY</strong> WILL HOLD A PUBLIC MEETING IN THE COMMISSION CHAMBERS - ROOM 1400 OF THE UTAH COUNTY ADMINISTRATION BUILDING 100 E CENTER ST, PROVO, UT 84606 <strong>May 27, 2026 - 2:00 PM</strong></p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/05_27_2026_CRA_Meeting_Agenda_b9253d97cb.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span class="sr-only"> about May 27, 2026 Utah County Community Reinvestment Agency Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-576" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">May 27, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">May 26, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="https://agenda.utahcounty.gov/agenda?id=1891" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda with Documents<span class="sr-only"> about May 27, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/Commission_Agenda_2026_05_27_14c10d6a23.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Agenda<span class="sr-only"> about May 27, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-566" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Utah County Sheriff&#x27;s Annual Awards</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">May 22, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Utah County Sheriff&#x27;s Department recently hosted its annual award ceremony, a carefully planned evening designed to recognize and celebrate the extraordinary, selfless dedication of the men and women who serve with the Utah County Sheriff’s Office. The event provided a formal platform to highlight the courage, professionalism, and tireless commitment demonstrated by deputies and support staff who, through long hours and challenging assignments, upheld the highest standards of public safety. </p>
<p>Nominations came from fellow deputies and colleagues across the office, reflecting a broad range of incidents and operations throughout 2025 in which teamwork, sound judgment, and compassionate service made a tangible difference in the community. In addition to deputy and staff recognitions, several local residents were honored for their decisive actions in emergency situations, their willingness to intervene, and their role in saving lives.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-557" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Election Notice</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">May 22, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Notice is given that a regular primary election will be held on Tuesday, June 23, 2026 to nominate party candidates for the parties and candidates for nonpartisan local school board positions listed on the regular primary election ballot.</p>
<p>For voters receiving a ballot by mail, ballots will be mailed beginning 21 calendar days before the election. The location of ballot drop boxes, the location and hours of polling places, including early voting polling places and hours, and qualifications of individuals who may vote in the election can be found at vote.utahcounty.gov/voting-locations and vote.utah.gov, by calling (801) 851-VOTE (8683), or at 100 E Center Street, Ste 3100, Provo, UT 84606. On the day of the election, the polls will open at 7 a.m. and continue open until 8 p.m. Attest: Aaron R. Davidson</p>
<p>Accessible voting machines will be available at all polling locations for those that need assistance when voting. More information on options for voters with disabilities can be found at: <a href="https://vote.utahcounty.gov/voter-information" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://vote.utahcounty.gov/voter-information<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>. If you have any additional questions, contact our office at (801) 851-VOTE (8683) or <a href="mailto:elections@utahcounty.gov">elections@utahcounty.gov</a>. </p>
<p>A public demonstration will be held to test the voting devices and tabulation equipment being used for the noticed election. Anyone who would like to witness the testing is invited to attend, beginning at 10:00 a.m. on May 28, 2026 at the Utah County Ballot Center - 100 E Center Street ROOM LL700 Provo, Utah. The Polling Locations listed below will be open from 7:00 a.m. to 8:00 p.m. on Election Day for any voter to vote in person.</p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/Election_Notice_Primary_385f6cfe14.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Polling Locations<span class="sr-only"> about Election Notice (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-581" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">New Amphitheater in Provo Canyon</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">May 19, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>PROVO, Utah — Vesper Amphitheater is planned for Provo Canyon, transforming an existing gravel pit into a year-round entertainment destination with indoor and outdoor capacity, officials said. The project aims to blend entertainment with the canyon’s natural setting while preserving the landscape for future generations.</p>
<p>The project is anchored by the belief that entertainment and nature can coexist without compromising the canyon’s character. It seeks to transform rather than overwhelm the landscape and to honor the canyon’s reverence.</p>
<p>Indoor capacity is planned for about 8,000 during winter configurations, with outdoor capacity up to 20,000 in the summer. Groundbreaking is anticipated in spring 2027. The venue is designed to offer Utah a setting unlike anywhere else in the Mountain West, with both indoor and outdoor spaces that complement each other across seasons.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-554" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Notice of Ballot Drop Box Locations</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">May 19, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>NOTICE OF BALLOT DROP BOX LOCATIONS</p>
<p>The Utah County Clerk, as the election officer, hereby provides notice of the locations of designated ballot drop boxes for the upcoming Primary Election in Utah County to be held on Tuesday, June 23, 2026.</p>
<p>A voter may utilize the following Utah County Elections Division resources to view or obtain a copy of all Utah County ballot drop box locations:</p>
<p>1.Visiting the Utah County Elections Division’s website: <a href="https://vote.utahcounty.gov/voting-locations" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://vote.utahcounty.gov/voting-locations<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a><br/>
<!-- -->2.Visiting the Utah County Elections Division in person during regular business hours at 100 East Center Street, Suite 3100, Provo, UT 84606<br/>
<!-- -->3.Sending a letter with your request to the Utah County Elections Division’s mailing address: 100 East Center Street, Suite 3100, Provo, UT 84606<br/>
<!-- -->4.Calling the Utah County Elections Division: (801) 851-VOTE(8683)<br/>
<!-- -->5.Emailing the Utah County Elections Division: <a href="mailto:elections@utahcounty.gov">elections@utahcounty.gov</a></p>
<p>More information can be accessed on the Statewide Electronic Voter Information Website at <a href="https://vote.utah.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://vote.utah.gov<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>.</p>
<p>The election officer will post on the Utah County Elections Division’s website, available at <a href="https://vote.utahcounty.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://vote.utahcounty.gov<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>, the location of each ballot drop box in Utah County, including any changes to the location of a ballot drop box and the location of additional drop boxes.</p>
<p>A voter may call the Utah County Elections Office at (801) 851-VOTE(8683) to obtain information regarding the location of a ballot drop box.</p>
<p>As required by law, this notice will be published at least 28 days before the day of the General Election, which will take place on Tuesday, June 23, 2026.</p>
<p>This notice fulfills the requirements of Utah Code § 20A-5-403.5(2)-(4).</p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/Notice_of_Ballot_Drop_Box_Locations_3a026dd0ca.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Notice<span class="sr-only"> about Notice of Ballot Drop Box Locations (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-546" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Notice of Early Voting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">May 19, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>NOTICE OF EARLY VOTING</p>
<p>All early voting for the June 23, 2026 Primary Election will take place at the Utah County Elections Office, located at 100 E Center Street Room 3100, Provo, UT 84606.</p>
<p>Early voting will take place each weekday beginning Monday, June 15, 2026 and ending Thursday, June 18, 2026, with an additional day the following week on Monday, June 22, 2026. Polls will be open between 8:00 AM and 5:00 PM each weekday.</p>
<p>More information about early voting in the 2026 Primary Election, may be obtained by any of<br/>
<!-- -->the following means:</p>
<p>1. Visiting the Utah County Elections Division’s website: <a href="https://vote.utahcounty.gov/voting-locations" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://vote.utahcounty.gov/voting-locations<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a><br/>
<!-- -->2. Visiting the Statewide Electronic Voter Information Website: <a href="https://vote.utah.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://vote.utah.gov<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a><br/>
<!-- -->3. Visiting the Utah County Elections Division in person during regular business hours at 100 East Center Street, Suite 3100, Provo, UT 84606<br/>
<!-- -->4. Sending a letter with your request to the Utah County Elections Division’s mailing address: 100 East Center Street, Suite 3100, Provo, UT 84606<br/>
<!-- -->5. Calling the Utah County Elections Division at (801) 851-VOTE(8683)<br/>
<!-- -->6. Emailing the Utah County Elections Division at <a href="mailto:elections@utahcounty.gov">elections@utahcounty.gov</a></p>
<p>As the election officer, the Utah County Clerk will post the early voting polling place, including any changes to the location of an early voting polling place and the location of additional early voting polling places, on <a href="https://vote.utahcounty.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://vote.utahcounty.gov<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>.</p>
<p>As required by law, this notice will be published at least 28 days before the day of the Primary Election, which will take place on Tuesday, June 23, 2026.</p>
<p>This notice fulfills the requirements of Utah Code § 20A-3a-604.</p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/Notice_of_Early_Voting_0c0664ba53.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Notice<span class="sr-only"> about Notice of Early Voting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-580" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">May 13, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">May 12, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="/api/media/file/05_13_2026_Commission_Meeting_Agenda_03dbb30ec5.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Agenda<span class="sr-only"> about May 13, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-565" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">May 13, 2026 Board of Equalization of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">May 11, 2026</p><p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-base text-gray-700">The public meeting will be held May 13, 2026 at 2:00 pm in the Utah County Administration Building, 100 E Center St, Room 1400, Provo, UT</p></div></article><article id="notice-564" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Notice of Logic and Accuracy Testing - May 28, 2026</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">May 11, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>NOTICE OF LOGIC AND ACCURACY TESTING</p>
<p>In accordance with Utah Code § 20A-4-104(1)(b), the Utah County Clerk will conduct a logic and accuracy test of automatic tabulating equipment prior to the Primary Elections to be held on June 23, 2026, for Utah County federal, state, and county races. The logic and accuracy test is open to the public and will be held on Thursday, May 28, 2026, at 10:00 AM Mountain Time at the following location:<br/>
<!-- -->Utah County Administration Building<br/>
<!-- -->Ballot Processing Center<br/>
<!-- -->100 E Center Street, Room LL700<br/>
<!-- -->Provo, UT 84606</p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/L_and_A_Public_Notice_3cd861bf57.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Notice<span class="sr-only"> about Notice of Logic and Accuracy Testing - May 28, 2026 (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-556" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Utah County Commissioners Pickleball Tournament Benefits the Children’s Justice Center</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">May 6, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Saratoga Spring, Utah - The second annual Utah County Commissioners Pickleball Tournament was a resounding success, bringing together players and supporters from across the county to stand behind child victims of abuse. Held at The Picklr Lehi, the event was more than just a day of friendly competition—it was a community rally to ensure that every child who comes to the Utah County Children’s Justice Center (CJC) has access to the healing services they deserve.</p>
<p><strong>Why this event matters</strong><br/>
<!-- -->The CJC serves as a soft-landing spot for children after traumatic experiences, guiding families toward the resources they need for healing. In recent years, the center faced a significant funding challenge when a notable portion of federal funding that financed therapy for kids was lost. The organizers’ goal for this year was to help replenish those critical funds and ensure ongoing access to trauma-focused therapy and other essential services.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-501" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Notice of Public Hearing - June 10, 2026</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">May 4, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="/api/media/file/Notice_2026_320_ec46f55e2d.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Notice<span class="sr-only"> about Notice of Public Hearing - June 10, 2026 (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-547" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Two banners rise at Provo Historic Courthouse to mark historic milestones</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">May 4, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Utah County has installed two new banners at the Provo Historic Courthouse, each celebrating a milestone of national and state history. One banner honors Utah’s Old Historic Courthouse, built 100 years ago, while the other commemorates the United States’ 250 years of independence. The banners offer a moment of reflection on the building&#x27;s history and the country’s founding, as residents pass by the courthouse in downtown Provo.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-579" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Anywhere you want to go…Fly Provo!</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 30, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Welcome to the Provo Airport!</p>
<p>So many exciting things are happening. The biggest one is expansion from four gates to 10 gates over the next two years. That will bring more carriers, more routes, more convenience to local residents, but also bring the economic driver of visitors and guests to Utah Valley. </p>
<p>The Provo Airport will never compete with Salt Lake International Airport, especially when it comes to non-stop flights, and they’re not trying to. That&#x27;s not why the Provo Airport was built.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-555" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Drive Thru Measles Vaccination Clinic</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 29, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Michael Leman, Nursing Director of the Utah County Health Department, drove us around as part of the department’s push to encourage vaccination in response to an uptick in measles cases in Utah County.</p>
<p>We&#x27;re doing a drive through measles vaccination clinic for the community. We started seeing cases about mid 2025 since that time, we&#x27;ve had 95 confirmed measles cases the entire state.</p>
<p>I believe we&#x27;ve surpassed 600 cases at this point; measles is a very, very contagious disease. It spreads very easily. It&#x27;s easily prevented with the measles vaccination.</p>
<p>The MMR vaccine has been around for a long time, very safe, very effective. Has about a 93% to 97% efficacy rate, and it&#x27;s the best thing you can do to keep yourself and keep your family protected.</p>
<p>If this at least gets people thinking about it, if they don&#x27;t come out today, it’s great if they talk to their primary care doctors about it. Obviously, the more people that if we can get vaccinated, the less we have to worry about it spreading.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-545" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Utah County Pickleball Tournament aims to Replace Funds for Children</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 24, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><strong>Saratoga Springs, Utah</strong> — In a community where neighbors know each other by name, a pickleball court is becoming a lifeline for Utah County’s most vulnerable children. The Utah County Commissioners are hosting a Pickleball Tournament to raise funds for the Children’s Justice Center (CJC), a one-stop, multidisciplinary hub that provides forensic interviews, medical exams, counseling, legal advocacy, and community resources under one roof to support children and families navigating abuse and trauma.</p>
<p>CJC serves nearly 1,000 new child abuse cases each year, and the center’s mission is clear: ensure comprehensive, trauma-informed care for every child who comes through its doors. This past year, the center faced a $50,000 reduction in federal funding that had been paying for therapy for child abuse victims. <strong>The Pickleball Tournament is critical to replacing those funds</strong> so children can continue to access essential therapy services. Funds raised will provide evidence-based, age-appropriate trauma-specific mental health treatment access for child abuse victims and their families.</p>
<p>This is more than a fundraiser; <strong>it’s a community commitment to healing and resilience</strong>. When children visit the center, caregivers and staff hear firsthand how meaningful compassionate care can be. A child recently shared, “I love how everyone does their best to talk to you and not just talk about one specific thing, it helps us feel more comfortable and feel safe to talk about everything….” a reminder of why these services matter. Another parent expressed gratitude for a guiding path forward: “Obviously we&#x27;re a wreck. Having people who know how to deal with this and who can give us a path forward is tremendously helpful. I am so incredibly grateful this exists.”</p>
<p><strong>What:</strong> Pickleball Tournament</p>
<p><strong>When:</strong> Friday, May 1, 8:00 a.m.- 6 p.m.</p>
<p><strong>Where:</strong> The Picklr, 629 Saratoga Rd Building 2, Saratoga Springs, UT 84043</p>
<p>Every serve supports the <a href="https://cjc.utahcounty.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Children&#x27;s Justice Center<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a> Whether you’re a pro or a beginner, come make a difference. Enjoy friendly competition and help ensure a brighter future for children.  There will be an opportunity drawing, auction items, networking opportunities, and a lot of fun.</p>
<p>Register Now: <a href="https://www.zeffy.com/en-US/ticketing/utah-county-commissioners-pickleball-tournament" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Utah County Commissioners Pickleball Tournament<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>
<p><strong>About the Children’s Justice Center -</strong> The CJC provides a multidisciplinary approach to addressing child abuse, offering forensic interviews, medical exams, counseling, legal advocacy, and community resources—all under one roof—to ensure comprehensive support for children and families navigating the complexities of abuse and trauma.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-500" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">May 13, 2026 Notice of Public Hearing</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 24, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>PUBLIC NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN THAT THE<br/>
<strong>BOARD OF COUNTY COMMISSIONERS, UTAH COUNTY, UTAH</strong><br/>
<!-- -->WILL HOLD A PUBLIC HEARING TO RECEIVE COMMENT ON THE DISPOSAL OF PUBLIC PROPERTY, IDENTIFIED BELOW</p>
<p>IN THE COMMISSION CHAMBERS, ROOM 1400<br/>
<!-- -->OF THE UTAH COUNTY ADMINISTRATION BUILDING<br/>
<!-- -->100 East Center Street, Provo, Utah<br/>
<strong>May 13, 2026 - 2:00 P.M.</strong></p>
<p>PROPERTY TO BE DISPOSED OF<br/>
<!-- -->Approximately 20.4 acres of real property near 6400 N 5050 W, American Fork, UT 84003.</p>
<p>PUBLIC COMMENTS</p>
<p>In compliance with the American with Disabilities Act, individuals needing special accommodations (including communicative aids and services) during this meeting should notify the Utah County Clerk&#x27;s Office at (801) 851-8113 at least three days prior to the meeting. Board members may participate electronically as needed, with the anchor location for the public meeting to be the same as listed above.</p>
<p><a href="/api/media/file/Notice_2026_325_7c01833253.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View Notice<span class="sr-only"> about May 13, 2026 Notice of Public Hearing (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-461" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Tricia Roger is Utah County&#x27;s April Employee of the Month</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 23, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Tricia Rogers, an invaluable member of the Public Works, is April’s Employee of the Month. Congratulations Tricia! In just over 1.5 years, she quickly learned systems and processes.  She owns her responsibilities and demonstrates professionalism and reliability for coworkers and the public.</p>
<p>She handles park reservations by phone with kindness, especially helping elderly residents navigate the system; an expert in Sportsites Parks Reservations program, she consistently goes the extra mile to resolve customer concerns and ensure their needs are met and is adept at resolving concerns, including Equestrian Park inquiries.</p>
<p>She supports the internal team and manages purchase orders for Fleet and Parks, keeping county accounts and P-Cards organized to keep operations running smoothly.  She is proactive and detail-oriented, she helped implement the new Event Permit program, collaborating with Information Systems and other departments.</p>
<p>Above all, Tricia brings positive energy, friendliness, and genuine care for others, contributing to a welcoming, dependable workplace.</p>
<p>Congratulations Tricia Rogers!  Utah County Public Works – April 2026 Employee of the Month.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-363" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">April 24, 2026 Utah County Criminal Justice Coordinating Council Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 23, 2026</p><p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-base text-gray-700">PUBLIC NOTICE IS HEARBY GIVEN THAT 
THE UTAH COUNTY CRIMINAL JUSTICE COORDINATING COUNCIL 
WILL HOLD A PUBLIC MEETING AT 51 SOUTH UNIVERSITY AVE., PROVO, UTAH 84601 IN THE HISTORIC COURTHOUSE, 3rd FLOOR COMMISSION CONFERENCE ROOM 
April 24, 2026 - 12 p.m. </p></div></article><article id="notice-352" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Safe Drug Disposal</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 21, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Protect our environment and community by properly disposing of your prescription medications at a permanent local medication drop box near you.  <a href="https://utahvalleydpc.org/safe-opioid-disposal/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Safe Drug Disposal - Utah Valley DPC<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a></p>
<p>Properly disposing of unused opioid medication prevents the risk of addiction, misuse, and overdoses. 75% of Utahans currently addicted to opioids get them from a friend or family member. You can dispose of almost any medication at these drop box locations, especially any over-the-counter or prescription medication (i.e. opioids or prescription cough medication).</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-75" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">April 22, 2026 Board of Commissioners of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 21, 2026</p><p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-base text-gray-700">The public meeting will be held April 22, 2026 at 2:00 pm in the Utah County Administration Building, 100 E Center St, Room 1400, Provo, UT</p></div></article><article id="notice-578" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">April 22, 2026 Utah County Reinvestment Agency Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 21, 2026</p></div></article><article id="notice-577" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">April 22, 2026 Board of Equalization of Utah County, Utah Public Meeting</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 21, 2026</p><p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-base text-gray-700">The public meeting will be held April 22, 2026 at 2:00 pm in the Utah County Administration Building, 100 E Center St, Room 1400, Provo, UT</p></div></article><article id="notice-563" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">HABs Happen </h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 17, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><strong>HABs Happen</strong></p>
<p>Provo, Utah — The Utah Lake Authority is launching a new media initiative called “HABs Happen” to better educate the public about harmful algal blooms (HABs). The campaign aims to equip residents and visitors with clear information and practical tools to understand what HABs are and what they are not.</p>
<p>Utah County Commissioner Skyler Beltran attended the media announcement and offered strong support for ongoing habitat and water quality work around Utah Lake.  “While algae is a natural part of lake ecosystems, it’s important to recognize the significant progress that has been made in recent years to mitigate it. In 2025, efforts to reduce phragmites and restore native vegetation achieved more progress than ever before. Those efforts are making a measurable difference and Utah Lake is now in its best condition in many years.”</p>
<p><strong>What HABs are and why they matter</strong><br/>
<!-- -->HABs are a naturally occurring cyanobacteria found in every body of water in Utah and throughout the United States. Their appearance can range from green pea soup to globules that resemble grass clippings. While ingesting HABs can cause illness, serious health effects are rare. HABs can be toxic for dogs, which should avoid contact with contaminated water. The campaign emphasizes that HABs are not an emergency every year, but rather a predictable seasonal occurrence that can be anticipated and managed with information and preparation.</p>
<p><strong>HABs Season – Know before you go</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Harmful algae blooms happen every year and are predictable</li>
<li>They are more likely to be at the end of summer when it’s hot</li>
<li>They are mildly dangerous to humans but toxic to dogs</li>
</ul>
<p>Commissioner Beltran wants Utahns to be mindful, take precautions but have fun when going outdoors.  “Utah Lake remains safe for recreational use, and I hope to see residents and visitors out enjoying it this summer.”</p>
<p>Starting in late July to early August, consistently hot temperatures create conditions for HABs to develop. While localized blooms can occur earlier in the year, the campaign encourages a “know-before-you-go” mindset—similar to precautions taken by skiers during avalanche season or hikers in the narrows. The public is encouraged to visit habs.utah.gov to see where HABs may be, and to avoid areas with blooms. The overarching message is simple: knowledge empowers safer, more enjoyable time on Utah’s waterways.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-575" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Drive-Up Immunization Clinic</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 16, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p>Free MMR (measles, mumps, rubella) vaccine clinic in Spanish Fork!</p>
<p>April 22, 2026<br/>
<!-- -->3–6 PM<br/>
<!-- -->Spanish Fork Fairgrounds (Ponderosa Building)<br/>
<!-- -->475 S Main St, Spanish Fork, UT</p>
<p>For kids (6+ months), teens &amp; adults not up to date. Limited supply—free while it lasts!</p>
<p>The MMR vaccine is a critical tool in preventing the spread of highly contagious diseases that can lead<br/>
<!-- -->to serious health complications. With measles cases actively being reported in Utah County and<br/>
<!-- -->throughout Utah, staying up to date on the MMR vaccine is an important step to protect yourself and<br/>
<!-- -->the community.</p>
<p>This drive-thru clinic is designed to make vaccination quick, easy, and accessible for individuals and<br/>
<!-- -->families. Attendees can remain in their vehicles while trained healthcare professionals administer the<br/>
<!-- -->vaccine safely and efficiently.</p></div></div></article><article id="notice-534" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Planning Commisssion Meeting Agenda</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 15, 2026</p><p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-base text-gray-700">Planning Commission Meeting Agenda and Public Hearing Notice for April 21st 2026 </p></div></article><article id="notice-535" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">CANCELLED _ April 21, 2026 Notice of Joint Public Hearing</h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 10, 2026</p><div class="mt-3 max-w-prose break-words content-flow"><p><a href="/api/media/file/2026_Joint_Commissionmeeting_cancellation_1d323401ea.pdf" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cancellation Notice<span class="sr-only"> about CANCELLED _ April 21, 2026 Notice of Joint Public Hearing (opens in a new tab)</span><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 -960 960 960" class="size-5 shrink-0" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M180-120q-24 0-42-18t-18-42v-600q0-24 18-42t42-18h279v60H180v600h600v-279h60v279q0 24-18 42t-42 18H180Zm202-219-42-43 398-398H519v-60h321v321h-60v-218L382-339Z"></path></svg></a></p></div></div></article><article id="notice-532" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">UC Tax Tourism Advisory Board Meeting </h2><p class="mt-1 text-gray-500 text-sm leading-5">April 10, 2026</p></div></article><article id="notice-533" class="flex flex-col @3xl:gap-10 gap-4.5"><hr class="border-0 border-t border-gray-400"/><div><h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-700 text-xl leading-7">Notice of Vacancies